### What are the core entities?

The core entities are `Board`, `Cell`, `Piece` (abstract), concrete piece classes, `Game`, and `Move`.

```java
package com.bank.chess.model;

public class Cell {
    private final int row;
    private final int col;
    private Piece piece;

    public Cell(int row, int col) {
        this.row = row;
        this.col = col;
    }

    public int getRow() { return row; }
    public int getCol() { return col; }
    public Piece getPiece() { return piece; }
    public void setPiece(Piece piece) { this.piece = piece; }
    public boolean isEmpty() { return piece == null; }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.chess.model;

public enum Colour {
    WHITE, BLACK
}
```

### How does the Piece hierarchy work?

The `Piece` is abstract with a colour and an abstract `canMove()` method. Each concrete piece implements its own movement rules. This polymorphism replaces a giant switch on piece type and satisfies the Open/Closed Principle.

```java
package com.bank.chess.model;

public abstract class Piece {
    private final Colour colour;

    protected Piece(Colour colour) {
        this.colour = colour;
    }

    public Colour getColour() { return colour; }

    public abstract boolean canMove(Board board,
                                     Cell from, Cell to);
}
```

```java
package com.bank.chess.model;

public class Rook extends Piece {
    public Rook(Colour colour) { super(colour); }

    @Override
    public boolean canMove(Board board, Cell from, Cell to) {
        // Rook moves in straight lines: same row or same column
        if (from.getRow() != to.getRow()
                && from.getCol() != to.getCol()) {
            return false;
        }
        return board.isPathClear(from, to);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.chess.model;

public class Knight extends Piece {
    public Knight(Colour colour) { super(colour); }

    @Override
    public boolean canMove(Board board, Cell from, Cell to) {
        int rowDiff = Math.abs(from.getRow() - to.getRow());
        int colDiff = Math.abs(from.getCol() - to.getCol());
        // Knight moves in an L-shape: 2+1 or 1+2
        return (rowDiff == 2 && colDiff == 1)
                || (rowDiff == 1 && colDiff == 2);
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.chess.model;

public class Bishop extends Piece {
    public Bishop(Colour colour) { super(colour); }

    @Override
    public boolean canMove(Board board, Cell from, Cell to) {
        int rowDiff = Math.abs(from.getRow() - to.getRow());
        int colDiff = Math.abs(from.getCol() - to.getCol());
        if (rowDiff != colDiff) return false;
        return board.isPathClear(from, to);
    }
}
```

### How does the Board provide path checking?

The Board provides a helper to check if the path between two cells is clear (no pieces blocking), which sliding pieces like Rook, Bishop, and Queen rely on.

```java
package com.bank.chess.model;

public class Board {
    private final Cell[][] grid;

    public Board() {
        grid = new Cell[8][8];
        for (int r = 0; r < 8; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < 8; c++) {
                grid[r][c] = new Cell(r, c);
            }
        }
    }

    public Cell getCell(int row, int col) {
        return grid[row][col];
    }

    public boolean isPathClear(Cell from, Cell to) {
        int rowDir = Integer.signum(to.getRow() - from.getRow());
        int colDir = Integer.signum(to.getCol() - from.getCol());
        int r = from.getRow() + rowDir;
        int c = from.getCol() + colDir;
        while (r != to.getRow() || c != to.getCol()) {
            if (!grid[r][c].isEmpty()) return false;
            r += rowDir;
            c += colDir;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

### How does move validation work?

Validating a move is a three-stage pipeline:

1. The piece confirms the geometry is legal for its type (`canMove`).
2. The path is unobstructed (already handled inside `canMove` for sliding pieces).
3. The move does not leave the mover's own king in check.

```java
package com.bank.chess.service;

import com.bank.chess.model.*;

public class Game {
    private final Board board;
    private Colour currentTurn;

    public Game() {
        this.board = new Board();
        this.currentTurn = Colour.WHITE;
        // setup initial pieces...
    }

    public boolean makeMove(Cell from, Cell to) {
        Piece piece = from.getPiece();
        if (piece == null) return false;
        if (piece.getColour() != currentTurn) return false;

        // Stage 1+2: geometry and path
        if (!piece.canMove(board, from, to)) return false;

        // Stage 3: does not leave own king in check
        Piece captured = to.getPiece();
        to.setPiece(piece);
        from.setPiece(null);
        boolean leavesKingInCheck = isInCheck(currentTurn);
        // undo temporary move
        from.setPiece(piece);
        to.setPiece(captured);
        if (leavesKingInCheck) return false;

        // Apply the move
        to.setPiece(piece);
        from.setPiece(null);
        currentTurn = (currentTurn == Colour.WHITE)
                ? Colour.BLACK : Colour.WHITE;
        System.out.println(piece.getClass().getSimpleName()
                + " moved from (" + from.getRow() + ","
                + from.getCol() + ") to (" + to.getRow()
                + "," + to.getCol() + ")");
        return true;
    }
}
```

### How does check and checkmate detection work?

Check detection asks whether any enemy piece can legally move to the king's cell. Checkmate is check plus no legal move that removes the check.

```java
public boolean isInCheck(Colour colour) {
    Cell kingCell = findKing(colour);
    Colour enemy = (colour == Colour.WHITE)
            ? Colour.BLACK : Colour.WHITE;
    for (int r = 0; r < 8; r++) {
        for (int c = 0; c < 8; c++) {
            Cell cell = board.getCell(r, c);
            if (!cell.isEmpty()
                    && cell.getPiece().getColour() == enemy
                    && cell.getPiece().canMove(
                    board, cell, kingCell)) {
                return true;
            }
        }
    }
    return false;
}

public boolean isCheckmate(Colour colour) {
    if (!isInCheck(colour)) return false;
    // Try every legal move for the colour
    for (int r = 0; r < 8; r++) {
        for (int c = 0; c < 8; c++) {
            Cell from = board.getCell(r, c);
            if (from.isEmpty()
                    || from.getPiece().getColour() != colour) {
                continue;
            }
            for (int tr = 0; tr < 8; tr++) {
                for (int tc = 0; tc < 8; tc++) {
                    Cell to = board.getCell(tr, tc);
                    if (tryMoveEscapesCheck(from, to, colour)) {
                        return false; // found an escape
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return true; // no escape: checkmate
}
```

### How does turn management work?

The `Game` strictly alternates white and black. Any attempt to move the wrong colour's piece is rejected.

```java
if (piece.getColour() != currentTurn) return false;
```

After a successful move, the turn switches to the other colour.

### How can moves be modelled as commands?

Modelling each move as a Command object enables undo, redo, and replay.

```java
package com.bank.chess.model;

public class Move {
    private final Cell from;
    private final Cell to;
    private final Piece movedPiece;
    private final Piece capturedPiece;

    public Move(Cell from, Cell to, Piece moved, Piece captured) {
        this.from = from;
        this.to = to;
        this.movedPiece = moved;
        this.capturedPiece = captured;
    }

    public void undo() {
        from.setPiece(movedPiece);
        to.setPiece(capturedPiece);
    }
}
```

### What about special moves?

Castling, en passant, and pawn promotion are special moves that break the standard validation pipeline. Handle them as explicit cases in the `makeMove` method or as separate command types.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Move validation and mate detection share the attack-computation logic. Compute attacked squares once and reuse for both.
- Modelling moves as Command objects gives undo, redo, and game replay for free.
- The piece hierarchy eliminates a giant switch statement. Adding a variant piece (like a fairy chess piece) is a new class.
- For AI, the Board must support efficient copy-on-move for search trees.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How do you handle castling and en passant?
- How do you detect stalemate versus checkmate?
- How would you implement undo/redo?
- How would you add a timer (blitz chess)?
- What is the time complexity of checkmate detection?
